  "articles": [
    {
      "title": "Kannada and Telugu",
      "body": "<p>Kannada and Telugu grew from the same parent script and separated only around the 1300s. Many letters are still close: compare Kannada <span lang=\"kn\">ಕ ಮ ವ</span> with Telugu <span lang=\"te\">క మ వ</span>. If you learn one, the other comes quickly.</p>\n<p>The clearest difference is the cap. Telugu letters wear a tick (✓) on top, Kannada letters a flatter, hooked bar.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "Second forms (ottakshara)",
      "body": "<p>When two consonants meet with no vowel between them, the second is written small, under the first. These are called <i>ottakshara</i>:</p>\n<table>\n<tr><td lang=\"kn\">ಮ್ + ಮ = ಮ್ಮ</td><td>mma, as in <span lang=\"kn\">ಅಮ್ಮ</span> (mother)</td></tr>\n<tr><td lang=\"kn\">ಪ್ + ಪ = ಪ್ಪ</td><td>ppa, as in <span lang=\"kn\">ಉಪ್ಪು</span> (salt)</td></tr>\n<tr><td lang=\"kn\">ಸ್ + ತ = ಸ್ತ</td><td>sta, as in <span lang=\"kn\">ಪುಸ್ತಕ</span> (book)</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>The subscript usually looks like the full letter without its cap. ರ is the odd one out: before another consonant it is written after it, as a small mark on the right (<span lang=\"kn\">ಧರ್ಮ</span>).</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "Kannada digits",
      "body": "<p>Kannada has its own digits, <span lang=\"kn\">೦ ೧ ೨ ೩ ೪ ೫ ೬ ೭ ೮ ೯</span>, seen on number plates and official signs in Karnataka, though international digits are more common.</p>"
    }
  ]
}
